$DIR/main.p:11:10: q holds a view of n (&*n.child, line 9), which was emptied by take(&pn.child) at line 10, so q can no longer be used here
    q looks at memory that n owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    &*n.child at line 9. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used
    only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 10 n
    was emptied by take(&pn.child), so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use q before line 10, or empty n only after the last use of q
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
